#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "21dc74fb-60a7-5a4f-a02b-34a300f59934" :question/math-academy-id "e-3858" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $y = 4\\sec\\left(2x-1\\right)-5$?" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form\n\n$$\ny = A\\sec(Bx +C) + D\n$$\n\nthe period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2$. Therefore, the period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{2}=\\pi\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "15a48902-1cdc-5e6f-b363-8fda4d62e3db" :question/math-academy-id "e-3859" :question/problem "Find an expression for the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = 2\\csc\\left(x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1$." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\csc x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\sin x$, and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of\n\n$$\n2\\csc\\left(x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1\n$$\n\nwe replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nx - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} =n\\pi\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - \\frac{π}{4} &= nπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{π}{4} + nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8dbb426-dd34-5686-bde9-3ef9d246b5e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ee6e62ca-7bc8-5c82-a852-973272e34152" :question/math-academy-id "e-3860" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $y = -2\\csc(3x+5)-1$." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\csc x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$[MATH: \\csc x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1, \\infty)]$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n$[MATH: \\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right) \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1, \\infty).]$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\leq -1, \\qquad \\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x)$, we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1$, we get\n$\\csc (3x + 5) \\le - 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) \\ge 2; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\ge 2 - 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\ge 1; f(x) \\ge 1$.\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1$, we get\n$\\csc (3x + 5) \\ge 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) \\le - 2; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\le - 2 - 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\le - 3; f(x) \\le - 3$.\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 1, \\qquad f(x) \\leq -3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $[MATH: f(x)\\in (-\\infty, -3] \\cup [1, \\infty).]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7c633805-c725-5591-8ce1-0dfcfb28a6d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3861" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x) = 2\\csc\\left(x+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\csc x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\csc x \\leq -1\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\csc x = -1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\csc x$.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(x+\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right) \\leq -1\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2\\csc (x + \\frac{3π}{4}) & \\le - 2 \\\\ f(x) & \\le - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2\n$$\n\nat the local maximum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ec59516-4ba6-5e62-b26a-bad36ea4448f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbd4995-8a45-5508-9d61-af6378c71029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082083} {:answer/id #uuid "e27be2f7-1260-557d-aac2-ecab760fdd6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082084} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0dfe6f-a665-50d0-94a9-0997567b6e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082085} {:answer/id #uuid "343e4f72-d26e-5a25-b6be-01a13365d006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082086} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7c1bef-6ae6-5868-8e8e-26a3d6ce94cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{nπ}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbd4995-8a45-5508-9d61-af6378c71029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082083} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f856ef-1f01-599f-afee-ba23b0932066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082082}] :question/id #uuid "b0bb2a84-7705-5755-83fd-22fc24ab86c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-65962" :question/problem "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = \\csc (4x + 1) - 2$? Assume that $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d217da5-3ce7-5177-ab96-d2115d1682c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082089} {:answer/id #uuid "9da6af30-8c82-502e-bc94-7e8c709d6d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082090} {:answer/id #uuid "cab1d192-b182-5540-b7d8-97a02a2b8289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082091} {:answer/id #uuid "287bb5c8-4962-53d0-99ff-f5827da0276b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082092} {:answer/id #uuid "42df44b8-b9d6-55ad-b4c8-9e5552e80daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "287bb5c8-4962-53d0-99ff-f5827da0276b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082092} :answer-field/id #uuid "83ce33d7-9cd4-5562-9f8b-bfd3f0af80b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082088}] :question/id #uuid "59e0e070-bfa1-5087-9d42-f4676cd92157" :question/math-academy-id "q-66028" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y = \\frac{1}{2}\\sec (3x + \\frac{π}{4}) - 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f679244-9174-5a3e-a2aa-cad4710f87b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082